REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w10","title":"Passives, participles, negatives, reported speech, conditionals","summary":"Five grammar lectures. Why a passive sentence hides the doer and how it is built (tense + be + V3); participles that describe nouns and gerunds that act as nouns; words like “anyone” and “a penny” that need a negative; how to report what someone said; and the zero, first, second, third and mixed conditionals.","n":10,"lectures":[{"code":"10.1","title":"Passives in english | structure, usage & rules for clear and effective communication","video":"Z63K3OugjUY","seconds":2124},{"code":"10.2","title":"Participles in english | usage, types & examples for clear and fluent communication","video":"7dx7qDB15Ts","seconds":1774},{"code":"10.3","title":"Negative sensitive elements in english","video":"SznTE3u_Tg0","seconds":1893},{"code":"10.4","title":"Reported speech","video":"KYeqK4c1Hig","seconds":1433},{"code":"10.5","title":"Conditionals in english structure & function explained with examples","video":"iZD4Fvg6bWs","seconds":1896}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"passives-hiding-the-doer","level":2,"title":"Passives: hiding the doer"},{"id":"what-a-passive-sentence-is-for","level":3,"title":"What a passive sentence is for"},{"id":"the-subject-is-the-place-of-focus","level":3,"title":"The subject is the place of focus"},{"id":"active-and-passive-side-by-side","level":3,"title":"Active and passive side by side"},{"id":"subject-and-agent-are-not-the-same-thing","level":3,"title":"Subject and agent are not the same thing"},{"id":"does-the-object-become-the-subject","level":3,"title":"Does the object “become” the subject?"},{"id":"how-a-passive-is-built-tense-be-v3","level":3,"title":"How a passive is built: tense + be + V3"},{"id":"only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive","level":3,"title":"Only transitive verbs have a passive"},{"id":"the-purpose-once-more","level":3,"title":"The purpose, once more"},{"id":"when-not-to-use-a-passive","level":3,"title":"When not to use a passive"},{"id":"participles-verb-forms-that-describe","level":2,"title":"Participles: verb forms that describe"},{"id":"six-sentences-to-start","level":3,"title":"Six sentences to start"},{"id":"two-types-ing-and-ed","level":3,"title":"Two types: -ing and -ed"},{"id":"participles-work-like-adjectives","level":3,"title":"Participles work like adjectives"},{"id":"gerunds-ing-forms-used-as-nouns","level":3,"title":"Gerunds: -ing forms used as nouns"},{"id":"negation-and-negative-sensitive-elements","level":2,"title":"Negation and negative-sensitive elements"},{"id":"learning-a-language-two-kinds-of-knowledge","level":3,"title":"Learning a language: two kinds of knowledge"},{"id":"learning-language-is-child-s-play","level":3,"title":"“Learning language is child’s play”"},{"id":"negation-the-tense-comes-out-of-the-verb","level":3,"title":"Negation: the tense comes out of the verb"},{"id":"words-that-need-a-negative","level":3,"title":"Words that need a negative"},{"id":"a-word-a-penny-a-red-cent-at-all","level":3,"title":"A word, a penny, a red cent, at all"},{"id":"questions-the-one-other-place","level":3,"title":"Questions: the one other place"},{"id":"reported-speech","level":2,"title":"Reported speech"},{"id":"why-we-report","level":3,"title":"Why we report"},{"id":"the-reporting-clause","level":3,"title":"The reporting clause"},{"id":"say-or-tell","level":3,"title":"Say or tell?"},{"id":"the-reported-clause","level":3,"title":"The reported clause"},{"id":"changing-the-pronouns","level":3,"title":"Changing the pronouns"},{"id":"changing-the-tense-backshifting","level":3,"title":"Changing the tense: backshifting"},{"id":"when-the-tense-stays","level":3,"title":"When the tense stays"},{"id":"conditionals","level":2,"title":"Conditionals"},{"id":"order-and-the-comma","level":3,"title":"Order and the comma"},{"id":"zero-conditional-facts","level":3,"title":"Zero conditional: facts"},{"id":"first-conditional-real-possibilities","level":3,"title":"First conditional: real possibilities"},{"id":"second-conditional-unreal-situations","level":3,"title":"Second conditional: unreal situations"},{"id":"if-i-were-you","level":3,"title":"If I were you"},{"id":"third-conditional-an-imagined-past","level":3,"title":"Third conditional: an imagined past"},{"id":"mixed-conditionals","level":3,"title":"Mixed conditionals"},{"id":"conditionals-for-politeness","level":3,"title":"Conditionals for politeness"},{"id":"all-the-types-together","level":3,"title":"All the types together"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passives</strong> (10.1): a passive sentence hides the doer (the <strong>agent</strong>) and puts what the action was done to in the subject position, the place of focus: <em>Classes were suspended</em>. Form: <strong>tense + be + V3</strong> (<em>were suspended</em>). The doer can come back at the end as an optional <strong>adjunct</strong> (<em>by the principal</em>). Only transitive verbs have passives. Active and passive are two separate sentences: the object does not “become” the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Participles</strong> (10.2): verb forms that are not the main verb and describe a noun, like adjectives: <em>a torn shirt</em>, <em>breaking news</em>, <em>baked potatoes</em>. “Present participle” (V + <em>-ing</em>) and “past participle” (V + <em>-ed</em> or V3) are only names: participles carry no tense. An <em>-ing</em> form used as a noun is a <strong>gerund</strong>: <em>Swimming is fun</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation</strong> (10.3): English has two negative words, <em>no</em> and <em>not</em>. In a negative sentence the tense comes out of the verb: tense + <em>not</em> + verb: <em>John likes novels</em> → <em>John does not like novels</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative-sensitive elements</strong> (10.3): <em>anybody, anyone, anything, any, a word, a penny, a red cent, at all</em> occur only with a negative: <em>John did not like anything</em>, but ✗ <em>John liked anything</em>. The <em>any</em>-words also work in questions; <em>a word</em> and <em>a penny</em> do not.</li>\n<li><strong>Reported speech</strong> (10.4): a reporting clause (<em>Raman said</em>) + a reported clause (<em>that he would join us</em>). Pronouns change to the reporter’s point of view, tenses move one step back (<em>is</em> → <em>was</em>, <em>have won</em> → <em>had won</em>, <em>left</em> → <em>had left</em>), and <em>tonight</em> becomes <em>that night</em>. No backshift for a present reporting verb (<em>He says …</em>), or for habits and facts (<em>the sun sets in the west</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Conditionals</strong> (10.5): an <em>if</em>-clause (the condition) + an independent clause (the result); a comma only when the <em>if</em>-clause comes first. Zero: present + present (facts). First: present + <em>will</em> (real future). Second: past + <em>could/would/might</em> (unreal; <em>If I were you</em>). Third: past perfect + <em>would have</em> + V3 (an imagined past). Mixed: past and present crossed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"passives-hiding-the-doer\">Passives: hiding the doer</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>The house was built in 1975.</em> This sentence tells us about the house and about the building work. It says nothing about the builder. That is what a <strong>passive sentence</strong> does: it keeps quiet about who did the action. Lecture 10.1 looks at both sides of the passive: what it is <strong>for</strong> (its function) and how it is <strong>built</strong> (its structure). If we understand how the mind puts these sentences together, the way a child learns them, we can use them freely: “We do not have to learn or memorize anything” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><a href=\"#/english2/w02/passives-how-they-are-built\">Week 2</a> built passives step by step. This lecture adds the reason behind them, and it corrects one popular rule.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-passive-sentence-is-for\">What a passive sentence is for</h3>\n<p>The slide “Some examples …”, with the passive part in red (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:50</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The passive voice <strong>is used</strong> frequently.</li>\n<li>The house <strong>was built</strong> in 1975.</li>\n<li>The road <strong>is being repaired</strong>.</li>\n<li>The building <strong>has been inaugurated</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer asks: why would someone say it this way? Say each sentence to yourself, write down its purpose in a corner of your notebook, and try to make its <strong>active counterpart</strong> (the matching active sentence) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The next slide has six more; the lecturer asks you to pause and colour the passive part yourself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The notification <strong>was sent</strong> to the employees.</li>\n<li>It <strong>was decided</strong>.</li>\n<li>The decision <strong>was taken</strong> last night.</li>\n<li>Letters <strong>have been dispatched</strong>.</li>\n<li>It <strong>has been reported</strong>.</li>\n<li>Classes <strong>were suspended</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The slide prints <em>Letter have been dispatched</em>; the lecturer adds the <em>s</em>, since <em>have</em> needs a plural subject.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Agent (the doer) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>agent</strong> is the doer of the action: the one who builds, repairs, sends or decides. In every sentence above we hear about the action and about what it was done to, but not about the agent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The house was built in 1975</em>: the house and the building, but not the <strong>builder</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>The road is being repaired</em>: the road and the repair, but not the people repairing it.</li>\n<li><em>The building has been inaugurated</em>: not who inaugurated it.</li>\n<li><em>The notification was sent to the employees</em>: the notification, the sending and the receivers, but not the <strong>sender</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“‘Who’, that is doer or agent, is not important in passive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The function of a passive: suppress agency (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A passive sentence has a clear intention: to <strong>suppress agency</strong>, that is, to hold back the information about the doer. “This is the main function of a passive sentence, that we do not want to talk about who did something” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-subject-is-the-place-of-focus\">The subject is the place of focus</h3>\n<p>Recall the template: a sentence has two parts, <strong>subject</strong> + <strong>predicate</strong>. The predicate holds the verb, with its objects and adverbs. The subject is the only part outside the predicate, which shows how important it is (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Lecture 10.1 adds one fact: <strong>whatever comes in the subject position becomes the focus</strong>, the centre of attention. In natural speech we put the focus on what comes first (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<p>So a passive does two things at once (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>it removes the focus from the doer;</li>\n<li>it brings the focus to <strong>what was done</strong>, by putting that in the subject position.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On the slide the lecturer circles the subjects: <em>The notification, It, The decision, Letters, It, Classes</em>. “They are the subjects of passive sentences,” and the rest of each sentence is its predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Passive sentence</th>\n<th>In focus (subject)</th>\n<th>Left out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The notification was sent to the employees.</td>\n<td>the notification</td>\n<td>who sent it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It was decided.</td>\n<td>it (the thing decided)</td>\n<td>who decided</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The decision was taken last night.</td>\n<td>the decision</td>\n<td>who took it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classes were suspended.</td>\n<td>classes</td>\n<td>who suspended them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“If something has been decided, then there must have been somebody who decided that. But we do not want focus there” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"active-and-passive-side-by-side\">Active and passive side by side</h3>\n<p>The slide “Comparative picture …” puts each passive next to an active sentence (P = passive, A = active) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Passive (P)</th>\n<th>Active (A)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Classes were suspended (by the principal).</td>\n<td>The principal suspended the classes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The notification was sent to the employees (by the owner).</td>\n<td>The owner sent notifications to the employees.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In the active sentence, <em>the principal</em> is the subject, <em>suspended</em> is the verb in the past tense, and <em>the classes</em> is the object of <em>suspend</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In the passive, <em>by the principal</em> can go at the end of the sentence, or we can drop it, because we do not want it in focus either. So it is an <strong>adjunct</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjunct</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>adjunct</strong> is an optional part of a sentence: it adds information, and the sentence is complete without it (<a href=\"#/english2/w03/complements-and-adjuncts\">Week 3</a>). <em>By the principal</em> is an adjunct in the passive: <em>Classes were suspended</em> is already a full sentence.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"subject-and-agent-are-not-the-same-thing\">Subject and agent are not the same thing</h3>\n<p>The slide “Sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subject + Predicate</li>\n<li>Predicate = Verb (objects) (adverbs)</li>\n<li>Active sentence: (1) The principal suspended the classes.</li>\n<li>Passive sentence: (2) The classes were suspended by the principal.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>(1) Active</th>\n<th>(2) Passive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject</td>\n<td>the principal</td>\n<td>the classes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the subject the agent?</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verb</td>\n<td>suspended (past, <em>-ed</em>)</td>\n<td>were suspended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>the classes</em></td>\n<td>object</td>\n<td>subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>by the principal</em></td>\n<td>(not there)</td>\n<td>adjunct</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“In the passive sentence, the classes is the subject of the sentence, but this is not the agent … the action is not being done by the subject” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). Most of the time the subject is also the agent, but they do not have to be the same (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). A later slide says it in one line: “Subjects = Agents [not true all the time]” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>). Its examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Raju wrote the letters.</em> The subject, <em>Raju</em>, is the agent.</li>\n<li><em>Letters were written.</em> The subject, <em>letters</em>, is not the agent: the letters wrote nothing.</li>\n<li><em>The book arrived in the parcel.</em> (Our reading: this sentence is active, since <em>arrive</em> takes no object, and still its subject did nothing; the book just arrived.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-object-become-the-subject\">Does the object “become” the subject?</h3>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A popular rule that the lecture rejects (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Many books say: “In a passive, the object of the active verb becomes the subject, and the subject becomes the object.” The lecturer says this is <strong>not true</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An object is a part that the verb requires. It cannot become a subject, and a subject cannot become an object (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>By the principal</em> is not the object of the passive sentence; it is an adjunct. So the active subject has not become an object (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In sentence (1), <em>the classes</em> is the object and <em>the principal</em> the subject. In sentence (2), <em>the classes</em> is the subject and <em>by the principal</em> is an adjunct, “period” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two separate sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Active and passive are <strong>two different sentences</strong>. We do not make the passive out of the active. The sentence is the basic unit of language, and every sentence is independent: it has its own subject and predicate, with a verb in the predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). The link between an active and a passive is in <strong>meaning and use</strong> (semantics and function), not in grammar (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the doer is not important, so something else is brought to the focus, and it takes the subject position;</li>\n<li>agency is suppressed.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">How this fits Week 2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Week 2 built a passive by moving the object up into the empty subject slot, warned that subject and object do not simply “exchange” places, and kept the grammatical subject apart from the agent (<a href=\"#/english2/w02/grammatical-roles-and-thematic-roles\">Week 2</a>). Lecture 10.1 goes one step further: as sentences, active and passive are independent. Both lectures agree on the form (<em>be</em> + V3) and on the optional <em>by</em>-phrase.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-a-passive-is-built-tense-be-v3\">How a passive is built: tense + be + V3</h3>\n<p>The passive <strong>morphology</strong> (the form of its words) is simple: tense, then <em>be</em>, then the V3 form of the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). The lecturer also writes it as <strong>be + en + V3</strong>: <em>en</em> stands for the participle ending, as in <em>writt<strong>en</strong></em>, <em>tak<strong>en</strong></em>; “an <em>en</em> gets added to the verb form and then we have the participle form” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Every verb has three forms. The slide “Passive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>V1 (present)</th>\n<th>V2 (past)</th>\n<th>V3 (participle)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>suspend</td>\n<td>suspended</td>\n<td>suspended</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>V2 and V3 of <em>suspend</em> look the same. That is why V3 is called the <strong>past participle</strong>: it looks like the past form, but it is a participle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The past form and the participle are different (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Only the present and past forms can be <strong>transitive</strong> (taking an object) or <strong>intransitive</strong> (taking none). In <em>The principal suspended the classes</em>, <em>suspended</em> is V2, a transitive verb in the past, so it takes the object <em>the classes</em>.</li>\n<li>A participle (V3) is neither transitive nor intransitive. <strong>Participles do not take objects.</strong> In <em>The classes were suspended</em>, <em>suspended</em> is V3.</li>\n<li>So <em>the classes</em> cannot be the object of <em>suspended</em> in the passive. It is the subject (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>). “In our mind, there is no confusion that classes has become subject; it is the subject” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where this rule stops (our note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Participles do not take objects” fits simple passives like <em>The classes were suspended</em>. It is not true of every V3 or <em>-ing</em> form: in the active perfect, V3 takes an object (<em>I have <strong>written</strong> a letter</em>); the <em>-ing</em> form in <em>was wearing a torn shirt</em> takes one (Lecture 10.2); and a verb with two objects keeps one in the passive (<em>Raju was <strong>given</strong> a book</em>). Use the rule to see why <em>the classes</em> is the subject of the passive, not as a rule for all participles.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Building <em>The classes were suspended</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Start from the meaning: something was done to the classes, and the doer is not important.</li>\n<li>Put <em>the classes</em> in the subject position, the place of focus.</li>\n<li>Keep the tense of the event, the past. The tense goes on <em>be</em>: <em>be</em> + past + plural subject = <em>were</em> (“<em>were</em> is also a <em>be</em> form of the verb”).</li>\n<li>Add the V3 form of the verb: <em>suspended</em>. As a participle, it takes no object.</li>\n<li>If you wish, add the doer as an adjunct at the end: <em>by the principal</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>The classes were suspended (by the principal)</em>: tense (past) + be (<em>were</em>) + V3 (<em>suspended</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two more examples from the slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Raju has been told.</em> <em>Tell</em> is a transitive verb, so where is its object? There is none, and “this is a good sentence”: <em>told</em> here is a participle.</li>\n<li><em>These letters have been written …</em>: <em>have been written</em> is be + en + V3, with the tense on <em>have</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A slip on the slide</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s full sentence is <em>These letter have been written a long time ago</em>. Two fixes: <em>letters</em> (plural, to agree with <em>have</em>); and <em>a long time ago</em> names a finished past time, which standard English puts in the simple past: <em>These letters <strong>were written</strong> a long time ago</em>. The passive pattern is the same: tense + be + V3.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive\">Only transitive verbs have a passive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Transitive verbs only (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>transitive</strong> verb takes an object (<em>suspend the classes</em>); an <strong>intransitive</strong> verb takes none (<em>sleep</em>, <em>go</em>). “Only transitive verbs participate in the passives.” Intransitive verbs have no passive counterparts. The lecturer’s examples cannot be made passive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I slept at nine last night.</em></li>\n<li><em>I went to Delhi last week.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer leaves the question <strong>why?</strong> open for you to think about (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>). A hint (ours, not the lecture’s): a passive puts into the subject position the thing the action was done to. An intransitive verb has no such thing, so there is nothing to bring into focus.</p>\n<p>Why bother? When the construction is clear, “the way it gets computed in our subconscious” is clear too, and we stop making mistakes. Flawless accuracy brings fluency, and “flawless fluency is what can actually give us confidence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-purpose-once-more\">The purpose, once more</h3>\n<p>The slide “Purpose of a passive sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Agency suppressed</th>\n<th>The agent added back as an adjunct</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The letters were sent.</td>\n<td>The letters were sent (by the manager).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The culprits were booked.</td>\n<td>The culprits were booked (by the court).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The meeting was adjourned.</td>\n<td>The meeting was adjourned (by the chairman).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>“Agent in a sentence gets the role of an adjunct.” We can add it at the end as extra information, or drop it, because “the whole purpose is not to reveal information about the doer” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Booked</em> here means officially charged by the police or a court; <em>adjourned</em> means stopped, to continue later. (The slide prints <em>The letter were sent</em>; <em>were</em> needs <em>letters</em>.)</li>\n<li>Doer and agent belong to <strong>semantics</strong>, which “simply means meaning”. So a passive has two parts to learn: its <strong>structure</strong> (tense + be + V3) and its <strong>function</strong> (suppressing the agent) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"when-not-to-use-a-passive\">When not to use a passive</h3>\n<p>The last slide, “Avoiding the use of passives”, leaves you two questions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z63K3OugjUY&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When do we use passive sentences?</li>\n<li>When shall we NOT use passive sentences?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The answer to the second “will also help you improve your writing”. From this lecture: use a passive when the doer is not important, or should stay out of focus. Week 2’s list of uses is in <a href=\"#/english2/w02/why-we-use-the-passive\">Why we use the passive</a>.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Turning the rule around (our suggestion)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If the doer <strong>is</strong> the point, keep the active. <em>The principal suspended the classes</em> says who is responsible; <em>Classes were suspended</em> hides it. When your reader needs to know who did something, or who must act, write the active.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"participles-verb-forms-that-describe\">Participles: verb forms that describe</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>A torn shirt</em>, <em>a sleeping tiger</em>, <em>baked potatoes</em>. <em>Torn</em>, <em>sleeping</em> and <em>baked</em> come from verbs (<em>tear</em>, <em>sleep</em>, <em>bake</em>), but here they do the job of an adjective: they describe a noun. Such forms are <strong>participles</strong>. Lecture 10.2 shows how to recognise them, why their names (“present”, “past”) have nothing to do with time, and what happens when an <em>-ing</em> form is used as a noun.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why study this? Basic communication in a new language is different from <strong>professional functioning</strong> in it. We need English in all our working areas: to express ourselves, and to understand what others say, “not only what is obvious, but also in between lines” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). That needs <strong>accuracy</strong> in building sentences. Accuracy leads to fluency, then to communicative competence, and finally to communicative confidence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"six-sentences-to-start\">Six sentences to start</h3>\n<p>The slide “Examples …”, with the participles in red (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Main verb</th>\n<th>The word in bold</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju was wearing a <strong>torn</strong> shirt.</td>\n<td>was wearing</td>\n<td>participle (from <em>tear</em>): describes <em>shirt</em>, like <em>red</em> in <em>a red shirt</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The house looked <strong>abandoned</strong>.</td>\n<td>looked</td>\n<td>participle: describes <em>the house</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju <strong>abandoned</strong> the house.</td>\n<td>abandoned</td>\n<td>the main verb; <em>the house</em> is its object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Looking</strong> at my condition, they felt sad.</td>\n<td>felt</td>\n<td>participle: begins a modifier clause, outside the main sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I was <strong>looking</strong> for an old book.</td>\n<td>was looking</td>\n<td>the main verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Running</strong> nose is not a good symptom.</td>\n<td>is</td>\n<td>participle: describes <em>nose</em>, inside the subject</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(The slide prints <em>Raju was bearing a torn shirt</em>. The lecturer says <em>wearing</em> works as well (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>), and <em>wearing</em> is the usual verb for clothes. The slide also drops an article: standard English says <em><strong>A</strong> running nose is not a good symptom</em>.)</p>\n<p>What the lecture draws from these examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A participle looks like a verb: a verb with a marker at the end (<em>abandon<strong>ed</strong></em>, <em>look<strong>ing</strong></em>), or a verb that changes its whole shape (<em>tear</em> → <em>torn</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not the main verb</strong>. In <em>Raju was wearing a torn shirt</em>, the main verb is <em>wearing</em>, a transitive verb whose object is <em>shirt</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li>It has an <strong>adjectival</strong> function (it works like an adjective): <em>torn</em> modifies <em>shirt</em>, just as <em>red</em> or <em>white</em> would (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). A word with an adjectival function is not the main verb of the sentence.</li>\n<li>The same word can be a participle in one sentence and the main verb in another: <em>The house looked abandoned</em> (participle; the verb is <em>looked</em>) and <em>Raju abandoned the house</em> (main verb) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Participle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>participle</strong> is a form derived from a verb that is not the main verb of its sentence. It may have an adjectival function: it <strong>modifies</strong> (describes) a noun. “Participle never functions like the main verb of the sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two uses of the word <em>participle</em> (our note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Grammar books also call the verb forms inside tenses and passives participles: <em>looking</em> in <em>was looking</em>, and <em>suspended</em> in <em>were suspended</em> (Lecture 10.1). There they are part of the verb, and the tense still sits on the helping verb (<em>was</em>, <em>were</em>). Lecture 10.2 keeps the name for the forms that describe a noun, and this section uses it that way.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The <em>-ing</em> clause at the front (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>Looking at my condition, they felt sad</em>: <em>they</em> is the subject, <em>felt</em> the verb, and <em>sad</em> a <strong>predicative adjective</strong> (an adjective after the verb that describes the subject). <em>Looking at my condition</em> is a clause, but not part of the main sentence: it is not the subject, not the predicate and not the verb. The lecturer calls it a <strong>modifier clause</strong> and leaves you two questions: what type of clause is it, and what does it modify? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>) A hint (ours): ask who was looking. <em>They</em> were, so the clause tells us more about <em>they</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The contrast pair (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>): in <em>Looking at my condition, they felt sad</em>, <em>looking</em> is not the verb of the sentence; in <em>I was looking for an old book</em>, <em>I</em> is the subject and <em>looking</em> is the main verb of the predicate. And in <em>Running nose is not a good symptom</em>, <em>running nose</em> is part of the subject, but <em>running</em> is not the subject: it modifies <em>nose</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"two-types-ing-and-ed\">Two types: <em>-ing</em> and <em>-ed</em></h3>\n<p>The six sentences show two types: participles with <em>-ing</em>, and participles with <em>-ed</em> or another past-looking form (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The next slide has only phrases (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>V + <em>-ing</em></th>\n<th>V + <em>-ed</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>breaking news</td>\n<td>baked potatoes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>working people</td>\n<td>roasted chicken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sleeping tiger</td>\n<td>fried vegetables</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>With or without a full sentence, every one of these participles works as an adjective for the noun that follows it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). The lecturer’s advice: notice such phrases when you read, write, speak and listen, and make a few sentences with them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Present participle and past participle (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Types of participles”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Present</strong>: V + <em>-ing</em> (<em>working</em>, <em>booking</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Past</strong>: V + <em>-ed</em> (<em>booked</em>), or the V3 of an irregular verb (<em>eaten</em>, <em>torn</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are only names, used to identify the forms. “They have very little to do with either present or past” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Participles carry no tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Tense means time. In a sentence, the information about time is “hosted on the main verb”, inside the predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). A participle is never the main verb, so it carries no time: <em>a sleeping tiger</em> fits a story about yesterday or about tomorrow. The lecturer calls this “the crux of the entire discussion”: understand this, and “we are done with participles”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why the names, then? The same slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>V1</th>\n<th>V2</th>\n<th>V3 [participle]</th>\n<th>V + <em>-ing</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>eat</td>\n<td>ate</td>\n<td>eaten</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>book</td>\n<td>booked</td>\n<td>booked</td>\n<td>booking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>present</td>\n<td>past</td>\n<td>“past participle”</td>\n<td>“present participle”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>V3 resembles V2 (<em>booked</em>, <em>booked</em>), so V3 was named the “past participle”. The <em>-ing</em> form was named the “present participle” in contrast (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). These are the terms of traditional grammar; the lecturer asks us to keep them apart from tense.</p>\n<h3 id=\"participles-work-like-adjectives\">Participles work like adjectives</h3>\n<p>The slide “Participles and Adjectives”: “Participles function as adjectives”, just as <strong>relative clauses</strong> do (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). A relative clause (<em>the book <strong>that I bought</strong></em>, see <a href=\"#/english2/w03/the-adjectival-relative-clause\">Week 3</a>), an adjective and a participle are all <strong>modifiers</strong>: they describe a noun (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide’s sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Running away from difficulties must not be appreciated.</em> The lecturer brackets <em>Running away from difficulties</em>, part of the subject, as a participle construction.</li>\n<li><em>Making portraits is certainly a good way of spending time.</em> He marks <em>making portraits</em> and <em>spending time</em> as participle uses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Cooked vegetables can be harmful.</em> <em>Cooked</em> modifies <em>vegetables</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not read these sentences for their message: they are out of context, only to show the forms (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Participle or gerund? (our note)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Cooked vegetables</em> is the clearest case of a participle describing a noun. In the other two sentences the <em>-ing</em> phrase is itself a subject (<em>Running away from difficulties …</em>, <em>Making portraits is …</em>), and <em>spending</em> follows the preposition <em>of</em>. By the next slide’s definition, an <em>-ing</em> form used as a noun is a <strong>gerund</strong>, and many grammar books call these gerund phrases. In a test, ask: does the <em>-ing</em> word describe a noun, or is it doing the job of a noun?</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"gerunds-ing-forms-used-as-nouns\">Gerunds: <em>-ing</em> forms used as nouns</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gerund (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Sometimes participles also function as nouns.” A verb + <em>-ing</em> used as a noun is called a <strong>gerund</strong>. The slide “Participles and Nouns (Gerund)”: “Verb + ing can be used as a NOUN.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>Swimming</strong> is fun.</em> <em>Swimming</em> is a noun, the subject; <em>is fun</em> is the predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em><strong>Running</strong> reduces risk of heart-attack.</em> <em>Running</em> is “a simple noun”, not an adjective for another noun.</li>\n<li><em><strong>Walking</strong></em> works the same way.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Adding <em>-ing</em> to a verb and using it as a noun is “a very productive pattern in English”: it works with a great many verbs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A gerund is no longer a verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When an <em>-ing</em> form becomes a noun, “they do not remain verbs”. If it is the subject or object, “there has to be something else functioning as the verb”: <em>is</em> in <em>Swimming is fun</em>, <em>reduces</em> in <em>Running reduces risk of heart-attack</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three questions for any <em>-ing</em> or <em>-ed</em> word</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Start from the template: subject + predicate, with a verb inside the predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7dx7qDB15Ts&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is it the main verb, carrying the tense? <em>I <strong>was looking</strong> for an old book</em> → main verb.</li>\n<li>Does it describe a noun? <em>a <strong>running</strong> nose</em>, <em><strong>cooked</strong> vegetables</em> → participle.</li>\n<li>Is it itself the subject or the object? <em><strong>Swimming</strong> is fun</em> → gerund.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"negation-and-negative-sensitive-elements\">Negation and negative-sensitive elements</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You can say <em>John did not like anything</em>, but not <em>John liked anything</em>. The word <em>anything</em> needs a <em>not</em> in the sentence. Lecture 10.3 calls such words <strong>negative-sensitive elements</strong>: they occur only in a negative sentence, that is, only when the sentence has a negative word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Your first language has such words too, and you learned them without any effort. The lecture uses them to show how finely the mind processes structure, and why an adult learner has to pay attention to it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"learning-a-language-two-kinds-of-knowledge\">Learning a language: two kinds of knowledge</h3>\n<p>The slide “Learning a language”: “Language acquisition (learning) involves both grammatical competence and pragmatic competence (performance) and they occur simultaneously” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grammatical competence</th>\n<th>Pragmatic competence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>also called competence, linguistic competence, I-language</td>\n<td>also called performance, linguistic performance, communicative competence, E-language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the processing of language <strong>inside</strong> our mind</td>\n<td>how we <strong>use</strong> the language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the rules of language structure</td>\n<td>the rules of language use</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The names are the lecture’s (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). Each has its own exact meaning, but for this class the lecturer groups them into these two sides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). Rules of use are “beyond grammar”, yet they go side by side with grammar (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). Learning is complete only when both come together (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"learning-language-is-child-s-play\">“Learning language is child’s play”</h3>\n<p>Language is a special <strong>endowment</strong> (a gift) of human beings. It is “one of the most sophisticated activities” of the human mind, and it develops from the input of the immediate society (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). A child learns it effortlessly; a grown-up has to put in effort, as we all do with English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). Hence the saying “learning language is child’s play” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Language Acquisition Device (LAD) and Universal Grammar (UG) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A normal human child is hardwired to acquire language from the immediate environment with the help of Language Acquisition Device (LAD) and Universal Grammar (UG).” Both are <strong>hypothetical</strong> parts of the human mind: we assume them to explain how children learn (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide’s diagram (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Input</th>\n<th>Human mind</th>\n<th>Output</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>from the immediate society (family, relatives): <strong>fuzzy and limited</strong></td>\n<td>LAD / UG</td>\n<td><strong>grammatical and infinite</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The input is fuzzy (unclear), not aimed at teaching language, and limited in quantity. Yet the output is “perfectly grammatical and infinite”: the child can make endless new sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The next slide: a complex system and a <strong>generative</strong> capacity (the power to produce new sentences) “miraculously develops” with the help of the LAD. The process is fast, effortless and needs no instruction. It recognises patterns, develops rules and produces a perfect system, called language. The result is a body of knowledge: <strong>Knowledge of Language (KoL)</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">What this means for an adult (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>What is effortless for a child “requires instruction” for an adult, which means it requires <strong>attention</strong>. It takes effort, and the processing is a little slower, though still very fast. That is why we must pay attention to structures (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). Negation and negative-sensitive elements are the lecture’s example of what to pay attention to.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"negation-the-tense-comes-out-of-the-verb\">Negation: the tense comes out of the verb</h3>\n<p>A sentence has two parts, subject and predicate, and the verb is the most important part of the predicate. When negation occurs inside the predicate, it makes the <strong>whole</strong> sentence negative (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negative words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English has two negative words: <strong>no</strong> and <strong>not</strong>. (These are the lecture’s two basic ones. Words like <em>never, nothing, nobody, none</em> are negative too: <em>Nobody said anything</em>.)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>John is a doctor.</em> An <strong>affirmative</strong> sentence: it says that something is so.</li>\n<li><em>John is <strong>not</strong> a doctor.</em> A <strong>negative</strong> sentence: <em>not</em> makes the whole sentence negative (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>“Negation always comes in a sentence around tense” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). Watch the tense in this example, written on the slide by hand:</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>John likes novels</em> made negative (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>John likes novels</em> is affirmative and in the present tense. The present tense and the singular number sit together on the verb: the <em>-s</em> shows the number; the tense is there but not visible (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li>To make the sentence negative, the tense has to come <strong>out</strong> of the verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Out of the verb, the tense and number sit on <em>do</em>: <em>do</em> + present + singular = <em>does</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Not</em> comes between the tense and the verb, and the verb is left bare: <em>like</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>John <strong>does not like</strong> novels</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The same happens in the past: <em>John liked novels</em> → <em>John <strong>did not like</strong> novels</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Order of elements in a negative sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>For negation to occur, the tense separates from the verb, and the negation comes <strong>between</strong> them:\n<strong>tense + negation + verb + objects</strong>: <em>did</em> + <em>not</em> + <em>like</em> + <em>novels</em>.\nWeek 6 used the same rule to build question tags (<a href=\"#/english2/w06/affirmative-and-negative-sentences\">Week 6</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-that-need-a-negative\">Words that need a negative</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Negative-sensitive element (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>negative-sensitive element</strong> is a word or phrase that can occur only when the sentence is negative, not when it is affirmative. “You have already used these things 100 times”; the lecture only asks you to look at them afresh (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide “Negative Sensitive Elements” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need a negative</th>\n<th>Do not need one</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>anybody, anyone (+ animate)</td>\n<td>somebody, someone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>anything (− animate)</td>\n<td>something</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>any</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a word, a penny</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>+ animate</strong> means used for living beings; <strong>− animate</strong> means used for non-living things (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Somebody, someone, something</em> are “not sensitive to negation”: they also occur in affirmative sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The words of the first group are built on <em>any</em>: that is how we get <em>anybody</em> and <em>anyone</em> (+ animate) and <em>anything</em> (− animate) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the slides, a star (*) before a sentence marks it as “not good”, that is, ungrammatical (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). This page uses ✗. The slide “Examples”:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Good</th>\n<th>Not good</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>John did not like anything.</td>\n<td>✗ John liked anything.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mary did not meet with anyone.</td>\n<td>✗ Mary met with anyone.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mary met with someone.</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In <em>John did not like anything</em>, the tense is already out of the verb (<em>did</em>), <em>not</em> comes after it, then the verb <em>like</em>, then <em>anything</em>. The presence of <em>anything</em> is “contingent upon” (depends on) the <em>not</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Mary met with someone</em> is fine, because <em>someone</em> is not negative-sensitive. <em>Anyone</em> “can occur in a sentence only with negation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li>One use the lecture does not cover (our note): when <em>any</em> means “it does not matter which”, the <em>any</em>-words are fine in affirmative sentences: <em>Anyone can learn to swim</em>; <em>Take any seat</em>. The rule above is about <em>any</em> in the sense “even one”.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"a-word-a-penny-a-red-cent-at-all\"><em>A word</em>, <em>a penny</em>, <em>a red cent</em>, <em>at all</em></h3>\n<p>Some phrases keep a special meaning only with a negative. The slide “More examples” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>With a negative</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Without a negative</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju did not speak <strong>a word</strong>.</td>\n<td>nothing at all</td>\n<td>Raju spoke a word.</td>\n<td>literally one word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarita did not give <strong>a penny / a red cent</strong> to beggars.</td>\n<td>nothing at all</td>\n<td>Sarita gave a penny / a red cent.</td>\n<td>literally one coin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarita did not speak <strong>at all</strong>.</td>\n<td>not even a little</td>\n<td>✗ Sarita spoke at all.</td>\n<td>not English</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Here <em>a word</em> does not mean one word: “metaphorically it means anything or nothing”. With that meaning it occurs only in a negative sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>A red cent</em> is an American phrase. A cent is the smallest unit of money, like our one paisa (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>). Without a negative, <em>a penny</em> or <em>a cent</em> means literally that coin, and the “nothing” meaning is gone (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>At all</em> also needs negation: <em>Sarita did not speak at all</em>, never ✗ <em>Sarita spoke at all</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>).</li>\n<li>(The slide prints <em>Sarita give a penny</em>; the past form is <em>gave</em>.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"questions-the-one-other-place\">Questions: the one other place</h3>\n<p>There is one context where some of these elements occur <strong>without</strong> negation, a “very unique” one: a <strong>question</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>). The slide “Replacing negation”:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Good?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you meet with anyone?</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td><em>any</em>-words: negation <strong>or</strong> a question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you get a penny? (= anything at all)</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td><em>a penny</em>, <em>a word</em>: negation <strong>only</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did Raju say a word? (= anything at all)</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“There is no negation. But it is a question sentence”, and <em>anyone</em> is fine there. The <em>a penny</em> and <em>a word</em> type “cannot occur even in question sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>). (Asked literally, about one coin or one word, these questions are ordinary English; the slide’s star is about the “nothing at all” meaning. Even in that meaning, such questions are heard when the speaker expects the answer <em>no</em>: <em>Did he say a word to you?</em> The lecture’s rule describes the plain, neutral question.)</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compare with your own language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer’s exercise: list the negative-sensitive elements of a language you grew up speaking, and compare them with the English ones. You learned yours effortlessly as a child. Seeing the same kind of pattern in English helps you notice other fine points of English structure, and it shows why an adult must learn them with attention and effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SznTE3u_Tg0&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"reported-speech\">Reported speech</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Raman says to you, <em>“I will join you for the meeting.”</em> Later you tell your team: <em>Raman said that he would join us for the meeting.</em> You have <strong>reported</strong> his words. You kept his message, but you changed <em>I</em> to <em>he</em>, <em>you</em> to <em>us</em>, and <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>. Lecture 10.4 explains these changes, and which verbs and clauses we use to report (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reported speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “What is reported speech?”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Reported speech is a way of reporting someone’s utterances without quoting their exact words.”</li>\n<li>It helps us report what other people said.</li>\n<li>It is also called <strong>indirect speech</strong>. The exact words inside inverted commas (“…”) are <strong>direct speech</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In structure, it is a sentence with <strong>another sentence embedded in it</strong> (placed inside it), and the embedded sentence is about what someone else said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Raman’s words (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Direct speech: <em>Raman said, “I will join you for the meeting.”</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep the reporting part: <em>Raman said</em>.</li>\n<li>Drop the inverted commas and join with <em>that</em>: <em>Raman said that …</em></li>\n<li>Change the pronouns to the reporter’s point of view: <em>I</em> → <em>he</em> (Raman); <em>you</em> → <em>us</em> (if you were among the listeners) or <em>them</em> (if you were not).</li>\n<li>Shift the verb one step back: <em>will</em> → <em>would</em>.</li>\n<li>Everything else stays: <em>join … for the meeting</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Raman said that he would join us / them for the meeting.</em> The lecturer sums it up: a change in the pronoun, a change in the verb form, and nothing else (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-report\">Why we report</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>To tell others what someone said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li>To <strong>soften</strong>. The reporter, the speaker of the moment, “has this authority to soften” the tone or intensity of what someone else said. This is a <strong>pragmatic</strong> function, a function of language use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The slide: “Reported speech has a significant role in communication as it aids in softening of language in formal contexts.” (Softening was the topic of <a href=\"#/english2/w06/softening-knowing-and-using\">Week 6</a>.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-reporting-clause\">The reporting clause</h3>\n<p>A reported sentence has two clauses: one <strong>independent</strong>, and one <strong>dependent</strong>, also called <strong>embedded</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reporting clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>reporting clause</strong> is the independent clause. It holds the <strong>reporting verb</strong>: <em>say, tell, ask, reply</em>, etc. These verbs are usually in the <strong>simple past</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Raman <strong>said</strong> that he would join us for the meeting.</em></li>\n<li><em>Alisha <strong>told</strong> me that she could swim when she was five.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why <em>told</em> in the second? We use <em>say</em> to report what someone said. We use <em>tell</em> when someone said it <strong>to</strong> someone: here, to the reporter, <em>me</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Reporting verbs” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Say</em> and <em>tell</em> are the most common reporting verbs.</li>\n<li>Others include <em>add, comment, complain, confirm, agree, admit, explain, offer, hint, suggest, advise, inform, announce, describe, elaborate</em>, etc.</li>\n<li><em>Say</em> and <em>tell</em> are used mostly in <strong>spoken</strong> language; the others mostly in more <strong>formal and written</strong> language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your job is to choose a verb that fits the context and does not change the meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). All of them are <strong>transitive</strong>: as verbs of the main clause, each takes the embedded sentence as its <strong>complement</strong> (the part it requires) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s exercise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Make a reported sentence with each verb on the list, and see what meaning each one creates. It gives you more words than <em>say</em> and <em>tell</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"say-or-tell\"><em>Say</em> or <em>tell</em>?</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Say</em> is used instead of <em>tell</em> to report <strong>questions, greetings and wishes</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>Tell</em> is followed by an object pronoun: <em>me, him, her, them, us</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct speech</th>\n<th>Reported speech</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rama said, “How are you doing?”</td>\n<td>Rama said how he was doing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ravi said, “Radhika is leaving for England tonight.”</td>\n<td>Ravi told me that Radhika was leaving for England that night.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Best of luck!”</td>\n<td>He wished her luck.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>How are you doing?</em> is a greeting-question, said directly to someone. With <em>tell</em> we would need a listener (<em>Rama told Rekha …</em>), and <em>Rama told Rekha how she was doing</em> “is not the appropriate context” for this verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). (Standard English reports a question with <em>ask</em>, and <em>you</em> becomes the listener: <em>Rama asked Rekha how she was doing</em>. The slide’s <em>Rama said how he was doing</em> would normally be read as Rama talking about his own health. <em>Ask</em> is on the lecture’s list too: see <em>The teacher asked if …</em> below.)</li>\n<li>In Ravi’s sentence, <em>tonight</em> becomes <em>that night</em>: the report happens later, so the time word moves, just as the tense moves back (<em>was leaving</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The slide’s P.S.: “In reported speech the message is more important than the exact words used by the speaker.” <em>Best of luck!</em> becomes <em>He wished her luck</em>: the exact words are gone, but the message stays.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>Tonight</em>, not <em>today night</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s footnote: say <strong>tonight</strong>, <strong>this morning</strong>, <strong>this evening</strong>. Never <em>today night</em>, <em>today morning</em> or <em>today evening</em>. Listen to people with a native-like command of English in formal settings: they do not use those forms. This “simple point … will add a lot of value to your sentences” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-reported-clause\">The reported clause</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reported clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The <strong>reported clause</strong> “represents what the original speaker said”. It is an <strong>embedded clause</strong>, and “its type depends on the reporting verb”. It is the complement of that verb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Four types, on two slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Used with</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>that</em>-clause</td>\n<td>most reporting verbs</td>\n<td>She complained <strong>that her neighbours were too loud</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>if</em> / <em>whether</em> clause</td>\n<td><em>ask, request, enquire</em> … (<em>if</em> is more common)</td>\n<td>The teacher asked <strong>if the children would visit the park</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>infinitival clause (<em>to</em>-infinitive)</td>\n<td><em>tell</em></td>\n<td>My father told me <strong>not to worry</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>infinitival clause (<em>-ing</em> participle)</td>\n<td><em>suggest</em></td>\n<td>He suggested <strong>completing and submitting the application letter the next day</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>An <em>if</em>-clause reports a request or a curiosity: someone wants to know something (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li>The slide’s P.S.: <em>if</em> is used for <strong>yes/no</strong> questions. For <strong>content questions</strong> (with <em>where, what, who</em> …) the pronoun changes, and the word order switches back to that of a statement. Rama’s question above shows both: <em>How <strong>are you</strong> doing?</em> → <em>Rama asked Rekha how <strong>she was</strong> doing</em> (more in <a href=\"#/english2/w06/complex-sentences-and-embedded-questions\">Week 6</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Completing</em> and <em>submitting</em> are the <em>-ing</em> forms of Lecture 10.2.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"changing-the-pronouns\">Changing the pronouns</h3>\n<p>The slide “Grammatical changes in reported speech, I. Modifications to subject and object personal pronouns” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The change of a personal pronoun, in subject or object position, “is contextual”: it depends on who is speaking about whom.</li>\n<li>In most cases the pronouns change to the <strong>third person</strong>, singular or plural: “I, we, me, my, mine, us, our changes to he, she, they, it, his, her, their, its.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide gives the two lists; the pairs below are ours:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>In the speaker’s words</th>\n<th>In the report</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I / me</td>\n<td>he or she / him or her</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>my / mine</td>\n<td>his or her / his or hers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>we / us / our</td>\n<td>they / them / their</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Example: <em>They said, “<strong>We have seen</strong> the Universal Studio.”</em> → <em>They said that <strong>they had seen</strong> the Universal Studio</em>. Two changes: <em>we</em> → <em>they</em>, because now someone else is speaking about them; and the tense moves back (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n<p>Three special cases (slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Direct speech</th>\n<th>Reported speech</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Your own words (the reporting subject is <em>I</em>): no pronoun change</td>\n<td>I said, “I watched the movie at the weekend.”</td>\n<td>I said that I had watched the movie at the weekend.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>You</em> changes to match the object of the reporting clause</td>\n<td>He said to her, “You look happy.”</td>\n<td>He said to her that she looked happy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third-person pronouns stay the same</td>\n<td>She said, “He lives in Chennai.”</td>\n<td>She said that he lived in Chennai.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>She said, “She is working on her project.”</td>\n<td>She said that she was working on her project.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“It’s not about just the rule, it’s about paying attention to what is being said to whom” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>). Look at each pair and ask who is speaking, who is reporting and what is reported; that keeps the pattern in your subconscious for a long time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"changing-the-tense-backshifting\">Changing the tense: backshifting</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Backshifting (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The verb tense in reported speech undergoes <strong>backshifting</strong>, i.e. the verb tense in the reported clause shifts to past tense.” The reason: the speech is reported <strong>later</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The shifts, each with the lecture’s example (slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>; the <em>will</em> row is the opening example):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Direct speech</th>\n<th>Reported clause</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>simple present</td>\n<td>simple past</td>\n<td>He said, “She <strong>is</strong> a nice girl.” → He said that she <strong>was</strong> a nice girl.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>present continuous</td>\n<td>past continuous</td>\n<td>She said, “He <strong>is leaving</strong> for Hyderabad.” → She said that he <strong>was leaving</strong> for Hyderabad.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>present perfect</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td>They said, “We <strong>have won</strong> the game.” → They said that they <strong>had won</strong> the game.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>present perfect continuous</td>\n<td>past perfect continuous</td>\n<td>I said, “I <strong>have been writing</strong> a book for two months.” → I said that I <strong>had been writing</strong> a book for two months.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>simple past</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td>She said, “We <strong>left</strong> our plant pots at home.” → She said that they <strong>had left</strong> their plant pots at home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>will</em></td>\n<td><em>would</em></td>\n<td>Raman said, “I <strong>will</strong> join you …” → Raman said that he <strong>would</strong> join us …</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-tense-stays\">When the tense stays</h3>\n<p>The lecturer moves quickly over the last two slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1296s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:36</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:40</a>). They give three rules:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Two readings.</strong> A report can describe (i) a completed action, or (ii) one that is still true at the time of speaking. For (ii) we use the simple present. <em>She said, “I go for a walk in the morning.”</em> → <em>She said that she <strong>went</strong> for a walk in the morning</em> (completed action) <strong>or</strong> <em>She said that she <strong>goes</strong> for a walk in the morning</em> (she still goes).</li>\n<li><strong>Present reporting verb.</strong> If the reporting verb is in the present, the reported clause stays in the present: <em>He says, “She is a smart person.”</em> → <em>He <strong>says</strong> that she <strong>is</strong> a smart person</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Habits and facts.</strong> The reported verb stays in the present when the event is habitual or factual: <em>She said, “The sun sets in the west.”</em> → <em>She said that the sun <strong>sets</strong> in the west</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>What changes, and how, “depends completely on the pragmatics”, our understanding of the context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). The lecturer’s warning: if we make an error while reporting, we sense it, or see it on the listener’s face, “but by that time it is too late”. So pause over these sentences now (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KYeqK4c1Hig&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four questions before you report (from the lecture’s rules)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Who is speaking now, about whom, and to whom? → change the pronouns.</li>\n<li>Is the reporting verb past (<em>said</em>) or present (<em>says</em>)? → past: shift the tenses back.</li>\n<li>Is the content still true, a habit or a fact? → you may keep the present.</li>\n<li>Has the time passed? → <em>tonight</em> → <em>that night</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"conditionals\">Conditionals</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>If Naina rings me, I will invite her for lunch at home.</em> The invitation happens only if the call comes first. A <strong>conditional</strong> sentence ties a result to a condition. Lecture 10.5 shows its structure (two clauses, and where the comma goes) and its types: zero, first, second, third and mixed. The lecturer’s advice: understand them, do not memorise them; let them settle in your subconscious, and your language will be impactful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conditional sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “What are conditionals?”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conditionals occur as <strong>complex sentences</strong>, in which the independent and subordinate clauses are joined by a conditional subordinating conjunction.</li>\n<li>The subordinate clause of a conditional is usually an <strong>if-clause</strong>. It represents the <strong>condition</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>independent</strong> clause represents the <strong>result</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A clause is <strong>independent</strong> when it can stand on its own. The conditional clause is not complete on its own, so it is <strong>subordinate</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). It is called conditional because it contains an <em>if</em>-type element (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which clause is which? (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Sentence: <em>I will invite her for lunch at home if Naina rings me.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>I will invite her for lunch at home</em> can stand on its own: “a good declarative simple sentence”. It is the <strong>independent</strong> (main) clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>If Naina rings me</em>, said alone, “gives a sense of incompletion”. It is the <strong>subordinate</strong> clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Together they form one sentence with two clauses: a <strong>complex sentence</strong>. “A bigger sentence, a complex sentence, is also a sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> main clause <em>I will invite her for lunch at home</em>; subordinate (conditional) clause <em>if Naina rings me</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>If</em> marks a <strong>precondition</strong>. The main clause becomes a possibility only when the subordinate clause materialises (comes true); if it does not, the main clause has no validity. So the conditional clause is subordinate, but “it determines the main clause” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"order-and-the-comma\">Order and the comma</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The comma rule (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The if-clause may precede or follow the independent clause. If it precedes, it is separated by a comma.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I will invite her for lunch at home if Naina rings me.</em> (<em>if</em>-clause second: no comma)</li>\n<li><em>If Naina rings me, I will invite her for lunch at home.</em> (<em>if</em>-clause first: a comma at its end)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Write the comma, and when you speak, make a small <strong>pause</strong> there: “it sounds better and in turn we sound impactful” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Name first, pronoun later (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>I will invite her … if Naina rings me</em>, the listener does not know who <em>her</em> is until the name arrives at the end. Both orders are grammatical, but with the <strong>name first</strong> the reference is easier and the listener “does not need to wonder”: <em>I’ll invite Naina for lunch at home if she rings me</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Likewise in <em>The clay becomes sticky if you add water to it</em>, the noun <em>clay</em> comes before the pronoun <em>it</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"zero-conditional-facts\">Zero conditional: facts</h3>\n<p>The slide “Types of conditionals: Zero conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It indicates <strong>general truths</strong> like laws, rules or general facts.</li>\n<li>It refers to a time that is <strong>now and always</strong>, and to situations that are <strong>real and possible</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The verbs in <strong>both</strong> clauses are in the <strong>present simple</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If you drive above 120 kmph on an expressway, you will be penalised.</em> (a rule)</li>\n<li><em>The clay <strong>becomes</strong> sticky if you <strong>add</strong> water to it.</em> (a fact; the main clause comes first, so no comma)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Adding water to clay and it becoming sticky … driving fast and getting penalised, these are facts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Check the verbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s rule is present simple in <strong>both</strong> clauses. <em>The clay becomes sticky if you add water to it</em> follows it exactly. The speeding example keeps <em>will be penalised</em> in the result clause; it is here because it states a rule. In an exercise, look for the pattern present simple + present simple.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"first-conditional-real-possibilities\">First conditional: real possibilities</h3>\n<p>The slide “Types of conditionals: First conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It talks about situations that have <strong>real possibilities</strong>: they can come true.</li>\n<li>The time referred to is the <strong>future</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>If</em>-clause: <strong>simple present</strong>. Independent clause: <strong>simple future</strong> (<em>will</em> + verb) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If the price of orange <strong>rises</strong>, demand <strong>will fall</strong>.</em></li>\n<li><em>The doctor <strong>will not consult</strong> the patient if she <strong>does not have</strong> her blood work done.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>“The main clause will always remain in future,” because it talks about what becomes possible once the condition is met (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"second-conditional-unreal-situations\">Second conditional: unreal situations</h3>\n<p>The slide “Types of conditionals: Second conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It refers to situations that are <strong>unreal, hypothetical or unlikely</strong>.</li>\n<li>The time referred to is the <strong>present or future</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>If</em>-clause: <strong>simple past</strong>. Independent clause: a modal auxiliary, <em>could / would / might</em>, followed by the infinitive (the base verb) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If we <strong>had</strong> an oven, we <strong>could grill</strong> the mushrooms.</em></li>\n<li><em>I <strong>might apply</strong> for a loan if I <strong>had</strong> to build a house.</em> (no comma: the <em>if</em>-clause comes second)</li>\n<li><em>If I <strong>had</strong> a lot of money, I <strong>would buy</strong> an airplane.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A past form, but not past time</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>If we had an oven</em>, <em>had</em> is a past form, yet the sentence is about <strong>now</strong>: we do not have an oven. The past form marks the situation as unreal. That is what the slide means by “the time referred to is present or future”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s real point is not to memorise “zero, first, second”. A conditional sentence carries one condition, which has to come true first; only then is the main clause true (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>). The order of the two clauses is free, but the verb forms change with how real or unlikely the situation is (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). Grammatically correct and contextually appropriate sentences are “key to confidence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"if-i-were-you\"><em>If I were you</em></h3>\n<p>The slide “Second conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>): when the verb in the <em>if</em>-clause is <em>be</em>, we use <strong>were</strong>, whatever the subject:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If I <strong>were</strong> you, I would fix the leakage on my own.</em></li>\n<li><em>If he/she <strong>were</strong> the captain, the team would win.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>“The ‘If I were you…’ construction is also useful in giving advice”: <em>If I were you, I would ask my sister to pursue higher studies</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>). <a href=\"#/english2/w06/suggestions\">Week 6</a> used it to soften a suggestion.</p>\n<p>The slide’s P.S.: the following are “also commonly heard”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If she <strong>was</strong> the president, no one would dare to cheat the public.</em></li>\n<li><em>The game would end soon if he <strong>was</strong> the opening player.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer then repeats the <em>were</em> sentences as the ones “we need to use” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>). In careful and formal English, use <em>were</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"third-conditional-an-imagined-past\">Third conditional: an imagined past</h3>\n<p>The slide “Types of conditional: Third conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It “refers to a situation that imagines a change in the past leading to a different result of that change”.</li>\n<li><em>If</em>-clause: <strong>past perfect</strong> (<em>had</em> + V3). Independent clause: <em>would</em> + the auxiliary <em>have</em> + the verb in its past participle form (V3) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>If I <strong>had prepared</strong> well, I <strong>would have scored</strong> good grades.</em> (I did not prepare well, and I did not score well.)</li>\n<li><em>We <strong>wouldn’t have lost</strong> the match if our fielders <strong>had played</strong> well.</em> (no comma: the <em>if</em>-clause comes second)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The whole sentence “remains a probability”: it imagines a past that did not happen (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Auxiliaries and tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer’s footnote: auxiliaries (helping verbs such as <em>would</em> and <em>have</em>) “do not indicate tense”, though they can be used in a sentence that has tense. He leaves this for another class.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"mixed-conditionals\">Mixed conditionals</h3>\n<p>The slides “Types of conditionals: Mixed conditionals” and “Mixed conditional” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>) give two kinds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th><em>If</em>-clause</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(i) a change in the past would have a result in the present</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td><em>would</em> + infinitive</td>\n<td>If I <strong>had not won</strong> the lottery, I <strong>would not be</strong> on a world tour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>(ii) a change in the present would mean a different past as well</td>\n<td>simple past</td>\n<td><em>would have</em> + V3</td>\n<td>If it <strong>wasn’t</strong> urgent, I <strong>wouldn’t have called</strong> you today.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>(i): in fact I won the lottery (past), so I am on a world tour now (present) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li>(ii): in fact the matter is urgent (present), so I called you (past). “A change in the present would mean a different past as well” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"conditionals-for-politeness\">Conditionals for politeness</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>If it wasn’t urgent, I wouldn’t have called you today</em> (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The situation: you call someone whom you do not usually call, and who does not expect your calls (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n<li>What the sentence says: you called because it was urgent for you to make the call.</li>\n<li>What it does: it tells the listener that you would not disturb them without a reason.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Conditionals can serve <strong>requests</strong> and add <strong>politeness</strong>. This form “makes your expression polite”, and it “can fetch a positive result” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>You may have heard that language has magic: it can get you the outcome you intend. The only “magic” is to use it properly, by letting these patterns run in your subconscious, without memorising them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>). Read carefully, too: careful reading has an impact on how you speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iZD4Fvg6bWs&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"all-the-types-together\">All the types together</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Situation and time</th>\n<th><em>If</em>-clause</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Zero</td>\n<td>facts, laws, rules; now and always</td>\n<td>present simple</td>\n<td>present simple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First</td>\n<td>real possibility; future</td>\n<td>simple present</td>\n<td><em>will</em> + verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second</td>\n<td>unreal or unlikely; present or future</td>\n<td>simple past (<em>were</em> for <em>be</em>)</td>\n<td><em>could / would / might</em> + verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third</td>\n<td>an imagined change in the past</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td><em>would have</em> + V3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed (i)</td>\n<td>past change, present result</td>\n<td>past perfect</td>\n<td><em>would</em> + verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed (ii)</td>\n<td>present change, different past</td>\n<td>simple past</td>\n<td><em>would have</em> + V3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Week 5’s lecture on <em>will</em> and <em>would</em> showed the zero to third types with other examples (<a href=\"#/english2/w05/conditionals\">Week 5</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passive</strong> (10.1). Function: suppress agency; the subject, the place of focus, is what the action was done to. Form: tense + be + V3 (be + en + V3): <em>Classes were suspended</em>. The agent may return as an optional adjunct: <em>by the principal</em>. In a passive the subject is not the agent. Active and passive are separate, independent sentences; the object does not “become” the subject. In a simple passive the participle (V3) takes no object. Only transitive verbs have passives (none for <em>I slept …</em>, <em>I went to Delhi …</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Participles</strong> (10.2): verb forms that are not the main verb; they modify nouns, as adjectives and relative clauses do: <em>a torn shirt, breaking news, baked potatoes</em>. Two forms: V + <em>-ing</em> (“present participle”), V + <em>-ed</em> or V3 (“past participle”). The names say nothing about time: tense sits on the main verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Gerund</strong> (10.2): V + <em>-ing</em> used as a noun: <em>Swimming is fun</em>, <em>Running reduces risk of heart-attack</em>. Then another word must be the verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Competence</strong> (10.3): grammatical competence (I-language: rules of structure, inside the mind) and pragmatic competence (performance, E-language: rules of use) develop together. Children: LAD/UG turn fuzzy, limited input into grammatical, infinite output, fast, effortlessly, without instruction (KoL). Adults need attention and effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation</strong> (10.3): <em>no</em>, <em>not</em>. The tense leaves the verb: tense + negation + verb + objects (<em>does not like</em>, <em>did not like</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Negative-sensitive elements</strong> (10.3): <em>anybody, anyone</em> (+ animate), <em>anything</em> (− animate), <em>any</em>, <em>a word, a penny, a red cent, at all</em> need a negative; <em>somebody, someone, something</em> do not. The <em>any</em>-words also occur in questions; <em>a word</em> and <em>a penny</em> need negation only.</li>\n<li><strong>Reported speech</strong> (10.4): reporting clause (independent; <em>say, tell, ask, reply</em> …, usually simple past) + reported clause (embedded: a <em>that</em>-clause, an <em>if/whether</em> clause, a <em>to</em>-infinitive or an <em>-ing</em> clause). <em>Say</em>, not <em>tell</em>, for greetings and wishes; <em>ask</em> for questions; <em>tell</em> + object pronoun. Pronouns change to the reporter’s view: mostly to the third person; your own words keep <em>I</em>; <em>you</em> follows the object of the reporting clause; third person stays. Backshift: present → past, present continuous → past continuous, present perfect and simple past → past perfect, present perfect continuous → past perfect continuous, <em>will</em> → <em>would</em>; <em>tonight</em> → <em>that night</em> (never <em>today night</em>). No backshift when the content is still true, when the reporting verb is present (<em>says</em>), or for habits and facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditionals</strong> (10.5): <em>if</em>-clause (condition, subordinate) + independent clause (result); a comma only when the <em>if</em>-clause comes first; name before pronoun. Zero: present + present. First: present + <em>will</em>. Second: past + <em>could/would/might</em> (<em>If I were you</em>). Third: past perfect + <em>would have</em> + V3. Mixed: past perfect + <em>would</em> + verb, or simple past + <em>would have</em> + V3. A conditional can make a request polite.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which of these can be made passive, and why? <em>I slept at nine last night.</em> / <em>The owner sent notifications to the employees.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Sleep</em> is intransitive: it takes no object. Only transitive verbs have passives, so <em>I slept at nine last night</em> has <strong>no passive</strong>.</li>\n<li><em>Send</em> is transitive (<em>sent notifications</em>). Passive: <em>The notification was sent to the employees (by the owner)</em>: tense (past) + be (<em>was</em>) + V3 (<em>sent</em>). The owner can stay out, or come back as an optional adjunct.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In <em>The classes were suspended by the principal</em>, name the subject, the agent and the job of <em>by the principal</em>. Has the object of the active sentence “become” the subject?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Subject: <em>the classes</em>, the place of focus. It is <strong>not</strong> the agent.</li>\n<li>Agent (the doer): <em>the principal</em>.</li>\n<li><em>By the principal</em>: an <strong>adjunct</strong>, optional extra information. <em>The classes were suspended</em> is complete without it.</li>\n<li>Lecture 10.1’s answer: no. Active and passive are two separate sentences. <em>Suspended</em> here is a participle, which takes no object, so <em>the classes</em> is simply the subject of this sentence, and <em>by the principal</em> is not an object.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Main verb, participle or gerund? (a) <em>Running nose is not a good symptom.</em> (b) <em>Running reduces risk of heart-attack.</em> (c) <em>I was looking for an old book.</em> (d) <em>The house looked abandoned.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Participle</strong>: <em>running</em> describes <em>nose</em>; the verb is <em>is</em>.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Gerund</strong>: <em>running</em> is a noun, the subject; the verb is <em>reduces</em>.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Main verb</strong>: <em>was looking</em> carries the past tense; <em>I</em> is the subject.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Participle</strong>: <em>abandoned</em> describes <em>the house</em>; the main verb is <em>looked</em>. (In <em>Raju abandoned the house</em>, <em>abandoned</em> is the main verb.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which sentences are good English, and why? (a) <em>Mary met with anyone.</em> (b) <em>Did you meet with anyone?</em> (c) <em>Raju did not speak a word.</em> (d) <em>Did Raju say a word?</em> (meaning “anything at all”)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) ✗ <em>Anyone</em> is negative-sensitive, and the sentence has no negative. Say <em>Mary met with someone</em> or <em>Mary did not meet with anyone</em>.</li>\n<li>(b) ✓ <em>Any</em>-words can also occur in questions.</li>\n<li>(c) ✓ <em>A word</em> means “anything” here, which is allowed because of <em>not</em>.</li>\n<li>(d) ✗ by the lecture’s rule: <em>A word</em> (like <em>a penny</em>) needs negation only; a question is not enough. Taken literally, “one word”, the question is ordinary English (and as a question that expects <em>no</em>, it is heard too).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Report <em>Ravi said, “Radhika is leaving for England tonight.”</em>, showing each change. Then report <em>She said, “The sun sets in the west.”</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Reporting verb: <em>said</em> → <em>told me</em> (the slide’s version: <em>tell</em> + object pronoun).</li>\n<li>Drop the inverted commas; join with <em>that</em>.</li>\n<li>Pronouns: <em>Radhika</em> is a third-person name, so nothing changes.</li>\n<li>Backshift: <em>is leaving</em> (present continuous) → <em>was leaving</em> (past continuous).</li>\n<li>Time word: <em>tonight</em> → <em>that night</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>Ravi told me that Radhika was leaving for England that night.</em> The sun sentence is a fact, so the tense stays: <em>She said that the sun sets in the west.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the type of conditional and its verb pattern: (a) <em>If I had prepared well, I would have scored good grades.</em> (b) <em>If the price of orange rises, demand will fall.</em> (c) <em>If I were you, I would ask my sister to pursue higher studies.</em> (d) <em>If I had not won the lottery, I would not be on a world tour.</em> Does <em>I might apply for a loan if I had to build a house</em> need a comma?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Third</strong>: past perfect (<em>had prepared</em>) + <em>would have</em> + V3 (<em>scored</em>): an imagined change in the past.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>First</strong>: simple present (<em>rises</em>) + <em>will</em> (<em>will fall</em>): a real future possibility.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Second</strong>, with <em>were</em> for <em>be</em>: unreal (I am not you), used to give advice.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Mixed (i)</strong>: past perfect (<em>had not won</em>) + <em>would</em> + verb (<em>would not be</em>): a past change with a present result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Comma: <strong>no</strong>. The <em>if</em>-clause comes second. A comma is needed only when it comes first: <em>If I had to build a house, I might apply for a loan.</em></p>\n</div></details>\n"});
